<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Transitional Government of Ethiopia</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – 1995 CE · capital Addis Ababa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The interim government formed by the victorious EPRDF in 1991, which accepted Eritrean independence and introduced Ethiopia's ethnic federal system.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peoples-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia</a> in May 1991, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front</a> (EPRDF) convened a conference in July 1991 that adopted a Transitional Charter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meles-zenawi">Meles Zenawi</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigray-peoples-liberation-front">Tigray People's Liberation Front</a>, became president of the Transitional Government.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The charter recognised the right of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia's</a> nations and peoples to self-determination, up to secession, and the country was reorganised into regions largely along ethnic lines. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo-liberation-front">Oromo Liberation Front</a> initially joined the government but withdrew in 1992. The new government accepted the independence referendum in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-eritrea">Eritrea</a>, which led to the creation of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> in 1993. The economy was opened to markets.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A constituent assembly adopted a new constitution in December 1994, and after elections in 1995 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia</a> was proclaimed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meles-zenawi">Meles Zenawi</a> as prime minister.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/transitional-government-of-ethiopia.jpg" alt="Transitional Government of Ethiopia" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Transitional_Government_of_Ethiopia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – 1995 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Addis Ababa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meles Zenawi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amharic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ethiopia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>TGE</dd></dl></aside></div>
